WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.390 --> 00:00:08.730
This is a video I’ve been excited to make
for a while. The story here braids together

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:14.180
prime numbers, complex numbers and pi in a
very pleasing trio.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:18.520
Quite often in modern math, especially that
which flirts with the Riemann zeta function,

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:23.590
these three seemingly unrelated objects show
up in unison, and I want to give you a peek

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:27.790
at one instance where this happens; one of
the few that doesn’t require too heavy a

00:00:27.790 --> 00:00:31.710
technical background.
That’s not to say it’s easy –in fact

00:00:31.710 --> 00:00:36.239
this is one of the most intricate videos I’ve
ever done– but the culmination is worth

00:00:36.239 --> 00:00:38.850
it.
What we’ll end up with is a formula for

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:44.870
pi, a certain alternating infinite sum.
This formula is actually written on the mug

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:49.520
I’m drinking coffee from as I write this,
and a fun but almost certainly apocryphal

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:54.730
story is that the beauty of this formula is
what inspired Leibniz to quit being a lawyer

00:00:54.730 --> 00:01:00.690
to instead pursue math.
Whenever you see pi show up in math, there’s

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:05.840
a circle hiding somewhere, sometimes very
sneakily. So the goal here is not just to

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:11.280
discover this sum, but to really understand
the circle hiding behind it.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.410
You see, there’s another way to prove the
same result that you and I will spend some

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:20.880
meaningful time building to with just a few
lines of calculus. But it’s one of those

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:26.680
proofs that leaves you thinking “Okay, I
suppose that’s true”, without a sense

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:32.380
for why, for where the hidden circle is.
On path you and I will take, what you’ll

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:38.469
see is that fundamental truth behind this
sum and the circle it hides is a certain regularity

00:01:38.469 --> 00:01:43.729
in prime numbers behave within complex numbers.

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:47.810
To start the story, imagine yourself with
nothing more than a pencil, some paper, and

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:54.259
a desire to find a formula for computing pi.
There are countless ways to approach this,

00:01:54.259 --> 00:01:58.670
but as a broad outline for the plotline hear,
you’ll start by asking how many lattice

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:04.140
points of the plane sit inside a big circle.
That question can lead asking about how to

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:10.149
express numbers as the sum of two squares,
which in turn will lead to factoring integers

00:02:10.149 --> 00:02:15.489
inside the complex plane.
From there, bringing in a function named chi

00:02:15.489 --> 00:02:22.060
will give us a formula for pi that at first
seems to involve a crazy complicated pattern

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:26.940
dependent on the distribution of primes, but
a slight shift in perspective will simplify

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:29.980
it dramatically and expose the ultimate gold
nugget.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:35.950
It’s a lot, but good math takes time, and
we’ll take it step by step.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:40.900
When I say “lattice point”, I mean a point
(a, b) in the plane where a and b are both

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:47.100
integers, a point where grid lines cross.
If you draw a circle centered at the origin,

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:52.690
say with radius 10, how many lattice points
would you guess are inside that circle?

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:59.400
Well, there’s one lattice point for each
unit of area, so the answer should be approximately

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:06.330
equal to the area of the circle, pi*R2, which
in this case is pi(102). And for a really

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:11.819
big radius, like 1,000,000, you’d expect
this to be more accurate, in the sense that

00:03:11.819 --> 00:03:17.480
the percent error between the estimate pi*R2
and the actual count of lattice points should

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:22.290
get smaller.
If you can find a second way to answer the

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:27.400
same question, how many lattice points are
in this circle, it might lead you to another

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:33.390
way to express the area of a circle, and hence
another way to express pi.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:39.680
So you play! And you wonder. And maybe, especially
if you just watched a certain calculus video,

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:45.819
you might try looking through every possible
ring that a lattice point could sit on.

00:03:45.819 --> 00:03:52.780
For each of those points, (a, b), its distance
from the origin in the square root of a2+b2,

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:59.209
and since a and b are both integers, a2+b2
is also an integer, so you only have to consider

00:03:59.209 --> 00:04:03.810
rings whose radii are the square roots of
whole numbers.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:12.500
A radius of 0 just gives that single origin
point. A radius of 1 hits 4 lattice points…

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:18.470
radius sqrt(2) hits 4 lattice points as well...sqrt(3)
doesn’t hit any lattice points...sqrt(4)

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:26.790
hits four again...a radius of sqrt(5) actually
hits 8 lattice points...

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:34.130
So what you need is a systematic way to count
how many lattice points are a given distance

00:04:34.130 --> 00:04:37.780
away from the origin, and to tally them all
up.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:43.670
If you pause and try this for a moment, you’ll
see that the pattern seems pretty chaotic,

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:50.190
which is a good sign that some very interesting
math will come into play.

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:55.450
In fact, as you’ll see, this pattern is
rooted in the distribution of primes.

00:04:55.450 --> 00:05:00.970
For example, look at the ring with radius
sqrt(25).

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:13.510
It hits (5, 0), since 52 + 02 = 25. It also
hits (4, 3), since 42+32 = 25, and likewise

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:21.010
it hits (3, 4).... and (0, 5)...
What’s really happening here is that you’re

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:29.670
counting how many pairs of integers (a, b)
have the property that a2 + b2 = 25, and it

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:34.470
looks like there’s a total of 12.
As another example, look at the ring with

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:40.250
radius sqrt(11). It doesn’t hit any lattice
points, which corresponds with the fact that

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:46.520
you cannot find two integers whose squares
add up to 11.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:53.310
Now, many times in math when you see a question
that has to do with the 2d plane, it can be

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:57.550
surprisingly fruitful to just ask what it
looks like when you think of that plane as

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:02.390
the set of all complex numbers.
So instead of thinking of this lattice point

00:06:02.390 --> 00:06:08.820
as the pair of integer coordinates (3, 4),
think of it as the single complex number 3

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:12.800
+ 4i.
That way, another way to think of the sum

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:20.900
of the squares of its coordinates, 32+42,
is to multiply this number by 3 - 4i.

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:24.430
This is called its “complex conjugate”,
it’s what you get by reflecting over the

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:30.430
real axis, replacing i with -i.
This might seem like a strange step if you

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:34.590
don’t have much of a history with complex
numbers, but describing this distance as a

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:40.380
product can be unexpectedly useful. It’ll
turn our question into a factoring problem,

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:44.750
which is ultimately why patterns among prime
numbers come into play.

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:53.150
Algebraically, this relation is straight-forward
enough to verify. You get a 32, then the 3*(-4i)

00:06:53.150 --> 00:07:04.130
cancels with the 4i*3, then you have -(4i)2,
which because i2 is -1 becomes +42.

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:09.820
This is also quite nice to see geometrically;
and if the following feels unfamiliar, I do

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:14.520
have another video where I go into more detail
about why complex multiplication looks the

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:21.750
way it does.
The number 3+4i has a magnitude of 5, and

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:29.890
some angle off the horizontal. Multiplying
by 3-4i rotates by that same angle in the

00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:35.860
opposite direction, putting it on the positive
real axis, then stretches by that same magnitude,

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:43.560
5, meaning you land on the number 25, the
square of the magnitude.

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:49.130
The collection of all these lattice points
a + bi, where a and b are integers, has a

00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:53.890
special name: the “Gaussian integers”,
named after Martin Sheen.

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:58.781
Geometrically, you will still be asking the
same question, how many of these lattice points

00:07:58.781 --> 00:08:05.330
(Gaussian integers) are a given distance away
from the origin, like sqrt(25). But we’re

00:08:05.330 --> 00:08:11.160
just phrasing it in a more algebraic way:
How many gaussian integers have the property

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:16.530
that multiplying by their complex conjugate
gives 25?

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:21.740
This might seem needlessly complex, but it’s
the key to understanding the seemingly random

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:26.590
pattern for how many lattice points are a
given distance from the origin.

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:33.880
To see why, we need to understand how numbers
factor inside the Gaussian integers.

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:39.240
As a quick refresher, among the ordinary integers,
every number can be factored as some unique

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:48.930
collection of prime numbers. For example,
2,250 can be factored as 2*32*53, and there

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:55.940
is no other collection of primes that also
multiplies to 2,250.

00:08:55.940 --> 00:09:03.650
Unless you just make some of the primes in
this factorization negative.

00:09:03.650 --> 00:09:10.450
So really, factorization in the integers is
not perfectly unique, it’s almost unique,

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:17.850
with the exception that some of the factors
might be multiplied by -1.

00:09:17.850 --> 00:09:22.280
Analogy with primes
Factoring works very similarly in Gaussian

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:26.780
integers.
Some numbers, like 5, can be factored into

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:36.410
smaller Gaussian integers, in this case (2+i)(2-i).
This Gaussian integer (2+i), cannot be factored

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:40.860
into anything smaller, so we call it a “Gaussian
prime”.

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:47.100
Again, this factorization is almost unique.
But this time, not only can you multiply each

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:54.840
of the factors by -1 to get a factorization
that looks different, you can also be extra

00:09:54.840 --> 00:10:04.030
sneaky by multiplying one factor by i, and
another by -1. This gives a different way

00:10:04.030 --> 00:10:11.200
to factor 5 into Gaussian primes.
But other than the things you can get by multiplying

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:20.340
some of these factors by -1, i or -i, factorization
within the Gaussian integers is unique.

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:25.670
If you can figure out how ordinary primes
numbers factor inside the Gaussian integers,

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:34.920
it’ll tell you all all other integers factor,
so here we pull in a crucial and surprising

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:38.990
fact.
Primes which are 1 above a multiple of 4,

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:48.700
like 5, 13, 17, can be always factored into
exactly 2 distinct gaussian primes.

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:52.990
This corresponds with the fact that rings
with radii equal to the square root of one

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:59.790
of these primes always hit lattice points.
In fact they always hit 8, as you’ll see

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:07.460
in a bit.
Primes that are 3 above a multiple of 4, like

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:15.340
3, 7, 11 and so on, cannot be factored further
in the Gaussian integers. Not only are they

00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:21.070
primes in the integers, they are also Gaussian
primes, unsplittable even when i is in the

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:24.220
picture.
This corresponds with the fact that a ring

00:11:24.220 --> 00:11:31.480
whose radius is the square root of one of
these will never hit any lattice points.

00:11:31.480 --> 00:11:40.060
This pattern is the regularity within primes
that we’ll ultimately exploit. And in a

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:44.930
later video I might explain why on earth this
is true; why a prime number’s remainder

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:50.320
when divided by 4 has anything to do with
whether or not it factors inside the Gaussian

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:57.910
integers, and hence, whether it can be expressed
as the sum of two squares. But here we’ll

00:11:57.910 --> 00:12:02.860
take it as given.
The prime number 2 is special, because it

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:11.510
does factor, as (1+i)(1-i), but these two
gaussian primes are a 90o rotation away from

00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:18.060
each other, so you can multiply one by i to
get the other. And that fact will make us

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:26.200
want to treat 2 a little differently for where
this is all going.

00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:31.240
Remember, our goal here is to count how many
lattice points are a given distance away from

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:37.260
the origin. Doing this systematically for
all distances sqrt(N) can lead us to a formula

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:40.730
for pi.
And counting the number of lattice points

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:47.450
with a given magnitude, like sqrt(25), is
the same as asking how many Gaussian integers

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:54.040
have the property that when you multiply by
its complex conjugate, you get 25.

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:59.130
So here’s the recipe for finding all Gaussian
integer with this property.

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:07.790
First, factor 25, which in the ordinary integers
is 52, but since 5 factors further as (2+i)(2-i),

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:16.830
25 breaks down into these four Gaussian primes.
Next, organize these into two columns, with

00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:20.360
conjugate pairs sitting right next to each
other.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:27.670
Now multiply what’s in each column, and
you’ll come out with two Gaussian integers.

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:32.279
Because everything in the right is a conjugate
to everything in the left, what comes out

00:13:32.279 --> 00:13:37.540
will be a complex conjugate pair, which multiply
to make 25.

00:13:37.540 --> 00:13:42.420
Picking an arbitrary standard, let’s call
that product from the left column the output

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:47.690
of our recipe.
Notice, there are three choices for how to

00:13:47.690 --> 00:13:55.120
divvy up the primes that can affect that output:
Here, both copies of 2+i are in the left column,

00:13:55.120 --> 00:14:01.830
and that left column product is 3+4i; you
could also have only one copy of 2+i is in

00:14:01.830 --> 00:14:08.680
the left column, in which case that product
is 5; or both copies of 2+i are in the right

00:14:08.680 --> 00:14:19.740
column, which will give an output of 3-4i.
Those three possible outputs are all lattice

00:14:19.740 --> 00:14:27.910
points on the circle with radius sqrt(25).
So, why does this recipe not yet capture all

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:32.110
12 lattice points?
Well, remember how I mentioned that a factorization

00:14:32.110 --> 00:14:38.160
into gaussian primes can look different if
you multiply some of them by i, -1 or -i?

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:51.530
If you write the factorization of 25 differently,
maybe splitting up one of those 5’s as (-1+2i)(-1-2i),

00:14:51.530 --> 00:14:58.410
it can affect our result.
But the only effect that would have is to

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:06.560
multiply the total output by i, -1 or -i,
so as a final step to our recipe, say you

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:12.410
have to make one of 4 choices: Multiply the
product from the left column either by 1,

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:16.070
i, -1 or -i.
The three lattice points we originally found,

00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:24.790
can each be multiplied by i, -1, or -i, and
that accounts for all 12 ways to construct

00:15:24.790 --> 00:15:29.400
a gaussian integer whose product with its
own conjugate is 25.

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:33.570
Extend to 53
The best way to see how this recipe counts

00:15:33.570 --> 00:15:36.339
lattice points more generally is to just see
more examples.

00:15:36.339 --> 00:15:44.240
If instead we were looking at 125, which is
53, we would have had 4 choices for how to

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:57.310
divvy up primes conjugate pairs into the two
columns, either including 0, 1, 2, or all

00:15:57.310 --> 00:16:03.060
three copies of 2+i in that left column.
Those four choices, multiplied by final four

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:11.100
choices of multiplying the product from the
left column by 1, i, -1 or -i, would suggest

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:19.110
there are 16 lattice points a distance of
sqrt(125) away from the origin.

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:25.020
And indeed, if you draw that circle and count,
you’ll find that it hits exactly 16 lattice

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:27.410
points

00:16:27.410 --> 00:16:32.530
If you introduce a factor like 3, which doesn’t
break down into the product of two conjugate

00:16:32.530 --> 00:16:37.080
Gaussian primes, it really mucks up the whole
system.

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:41.740
When you’re divvying up primes between the
two columns, there’s no way to split up

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:47.910
that 3. No matter where you put it, it leaves
the columns imbalanced, and when you take

00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:54.130
the product of all the numbers in each column
you wouldn’t end up with a conjugate pair.

00:16:54.130 --> 00:17:02.830
So for a number like 3*53, which is 375, there’s
actually no lattice point you hit; no Gaussian

00:17:02.830 --> 00:17:05.809
integer whose product with its own conjugate
gives 375.

00:17:05.809 --> 00:17:11.650
Extend to 32*53
But if you add a second factor of 3, then

00:17:11.650 --> 00:17:18.090
you have an option. You can throw one 3 in
the left column, and the other in the right.

00:17:18.090 --> 00:17:22.790
Since 3 is its own complex conjugate, this
keeps things balanced, in the sense that the

00:17:22.790 --> 00:17:29.690
products of the left and right columns will
be complex conjugates of each other.

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:34.260
But it doesn’t add any new options. There
will still be a total of 4 choices for how

00:17:34.260 --> 00:17:39.900
to divvy up those factors if 5, multiplied
by the final 4 choices of multiplying by 1,

00:17:39.900 --> 00:17:46.530
i, -1 or -i.
So just like the sqrt(125) circle, this guy

00:17:46.530 --> 00:17:50.490
also hits exactly 16 lattice points.

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:54.980
Let’s just sum up where we are.
When you’re counting how many lattice points

00:17:54.980 --> 00:18:01.190
lie on a circle with radius sqrt(N), the first
step is to factor N.

00:18:01.190 --> 00:18:09.010
For prime factors like 5, 13, and 17, which
factor into a conjugate pair of Gaussian primes,

00:18:09.010 --> 00:18:14.210
the number of choices you have is one more
than the exponent that shows up with that

00:18:14.210 --> 00:18:21.330
factor.
For prime factors like 3, 7 and 11, which

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:26.410
are already Gaussian primes and can’t be
split, if they show up with an even power,

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:30.900
you have one and only one choice for what
to do with them. If it’s an odd exponent,

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:36.960
you’re screwed, you have zero choices.
And no matter what, you have those 4 choices

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:38.360
at the end.

00:18:38.360 --> 00:18:45.370
By the way, this process right here is, I
think, the most complicated part of the video.

00:18:45.370 --> 00:18:49.890
It took me a couple times to think through
that yes, this is a valid way to count lattice

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:53.940
points, so don’t be shy if you want to pause
and scribble things down to get a feel for

00:18:53.940 --> 00:18:58.500
it.
The one last thing to mention is how factors

00:18:58.500 --> 00:19:01.200
of 2 affect the count.

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:10.260
If your number is even, the factor of 2 breaks
down as (1+i)(1-i), so you can divvy up that

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:16.410
conjugate pair between the columns. At first
it might look like this doubles your options,

00:19:16.410 --> 00:19:21.220
depending on how you choose divvy these up
between the columns.

00:19:21.220 --> 00:19:27.100
However, since multiplying one of these gaussian
primes by i gives you the other one, if you

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:32.860
swap them between the columns, the effect
on the output from the left column is to multiply

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:39.490
it by i or -i.
So this is redundant with that final step,

00:19:39.490 --> 00:19:44.750
where we take the product of the left column
and choose to multiply it either by 1, i,

00:19:44.750 --> 00:19:50.000
-1 or -i.
This means a factor of 2, or any power of

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:56.450
2, doesn’t actually change the count at
all; it doesn’t hurt, it doesn’t help.

00:19:56.450 --> 00:20:07.150
For example, a circle with radius sqrt(5)
hits 8 points, and one with radius sqrt(10)

00:20:07.150 --> 00:20:12.550
also hits 8 points, as does sqrt(20)... and
sqrt(40). Factors of 2 just don’t make a

00:20:12.550 --> 00:20:14.350
difference.

00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:19.020
What’s about to happen is number theory
at its best.

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:23.410
We have this complicated recipe telling us
how many lattice points sit on a circle with

00:20:23.410 --> 00:20:28.520
radius sqrt(N), and it depends on the prime
factorization of N.

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:35.000
To turn it into something simpler, we’re
going to exploit the regularity of prime numbers

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:40.450
that those which are 1 above a multiple of
4 split into distinct gaussian prime factors,

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:43.720
while those that are 3 above a multiple of
4 cannot be split.

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:46.950
Introduce chi
To do this, we’ll bring in a simple function,

00:20:46.950 --> 00:20:53.150
which I’ll label with the greek letter “chi”.
For inputs 1 above a multiple of 4, the output

00:20:53.150 --> 00:21:04.240
of chi is 1. For inputs 3 above a multiple
of 4, it outputs -1. And for even numbers,

00:21:04.240 --> 00:21:12.770
it gives 0.
So if you evaluate chi on the natural numbers,

00:21:12.770 --> 00:21:22.420
it gives this nice cyclic pattern 1, 0, -1,
0, and repeat.

00:21:22.420 --> 00:21:27.601
Chi has a special property, it’s what’s
called a “multiplicative” function. If

00:21:27.601 --> 00:21:34.850
you evaluate it on two number and multiply,
like chi(3)*chi(5), the result is the same

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:40.130
as chi evaluated on the product of those two
numbers, in this case chi(15). Likewise, chi(5)*chi(5)

00:21:40.130 --> 00:21:45.890
= chi(25)...and this works for any two numbers,
go ahead an try it.

00:21:45.890 --> 00:21:52.440
Show answer to counting question in terms
of chi

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:57.809
So, for our central question of counting lattice
points in this way that involves factoring

00:21:57.809 --> 00:22:04.210
a number, I’m going to write the number
of choices we have using chi in what at first

00:22:04.210 --> 00:22:09.160
seems to be a more complicated way, but it
has the benefit of treating all prime factors

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:14.430
the same way.
For each prime power, like 53, write (chi(1)

00:22:14.430 --> 00:22:23.380
+ chi(5) + chi(52) + chi(53)), adding up the
value of chi on all the power of this prime

00:22:23.380 --> 00:22:31.260
up to the one that shows up in the factorization.
Since 5 is 1 above a multiple of 4, all of

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:36.770
these are just 1, so this sum comes out to
4, which reflects the fact that the factor

00:22:36.770 --> 00:22:43.080
of 53 gives us 4 options for how to divvy
up its two Gaussian prime factors between

00:22:43.080 --> 00:22:51.299
the columns.
For something like 34, this looks like (chi(1)

00:22:51.299 --> 00:23:04.760
+ chi(3) + chi(32) + etc.). Since chi(3) is
-1, this sum oscillates, 1 - 1 + 1, etc. If

00:23:04.760 --> 00:23:10.480
it’s an even power, like 4 in this case,
the sum comes out to 1, which encapsulates

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:16.550
the fact that there is only 1 choice for what
to do with those unsplittable 3’s. If it’s

00:23:16.550 --> 00:23:25.250
an odd power, that sum is 0, indicating that
we have no choices.

00:23:25.250 --> 00:23:36.110
For powers of two, this looks like (1 + 0
+ 0 + etc.), indicating that with a factor

00:23:36.110 --> 00:23:41.510
of 2, we always have 1 choice for what to
do with it, it neither helps nor hurts the

00:23:41.510 --> 00:23:44.419
lattice point cause.
And as always, that 4 in front reflects the

00:23:44.419 --> 00:23:49.241
final choice of multiplying the output by
1, i, -1 or -i.

00:23:49.241 --> 00:23:52.809
We’re getting close to the culmination now,
things are starting to look organized, so

00:23:52.809 --> 00:23:57.610
take a moment, pause and ponder, make sure
this all feels good.

00:23:57.610 --> 00:24:05.240
Take the number 45 as an example, which factors
as 325. The expression for the number of lattice

00:24:05.240 --> 00:24:15.970
points is 4*(1 + chi(3) + chi(32))(1 + chi(5)),
which is the same as 4*(1 choice for what

00:24:15.970 --> 00:24:21.720
to do with the 3’s)*(2 choices for how to
divvy up the Gaussian prime factors of 5).

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:26.790
It might seem like expanding out this sum
is really complicated, because it involves

00:24:26.790 --> 00:24:31.420
looking at all possible combinations of these
prime factors.

00:24:31.420 --> 00:24:38.070
But because chi is multiplicative, each one
of these combinations corresponds to a divisor

00:24:38.070 --> 00:24:52.580
of 45, so really this looks like 4*(chi(1)
+ chi(3) + chi(5) + chi(9) + chi(15) + chi(45)).

00:24:52.580 --> 00:24:59.040
This covers every number which divides evenly
into 45, once, and only once.

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:03.260
And it works like that for any number, there’s
nothing special about 45.

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:08.910
That’s pretty interesting, and I think wholly
unexpected. This question of counting the

00:25:08.910 --> 00:25:14.250
number of lattice points a distance sqrt(N)
away from the origin involves adding up the

00:25:14.250 --> 00:25:18.190
value of this simple function over all divisors
of N.

00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:24.929
Now, remember why we’re doing all of this.
The total number of lattice points inside

00:25:24.929 --> 00:25:29.690
a big circle with radius R should be about
pi*R2.

00:25:29.690 --> 00:25:33.850
But on the other hand, we can count those
same lattice points by looking through all

00:25:33.850 --> 00:25:39.780
numbers N between 0 and R2 and counting how
many lattice points are a distance sqrt(N)

00:25:39.780 --> 00:25:43.630
from the origin.
We’ll go ahead and ignore the origin dot,

00:25:43.630 --> 00:25:48.340
with radius 0, since it doesn’t follow the
pattern of the rest, and one little dot won’t

00:25:48.340 --> 00:25:52.460
make a difference as we let R grow towards
infinity.

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:58.130
From all of this Gaussian integer and factoring
and chi function stuff we’ve been doing,

00:25:58.130 --> 00:26:05.559
the answer for each N looks like adding up
the value of chi on every divisor of N, and

00:26:05.559 --> 00:26:10.100
multiplying by 4.
Let’s just put that 4 in the corner for

00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:12.800
now and remember to bring it back in a moment.
Rearrange sum

00:26:12.800 --> 00:26:20.150
At first, adding up the values of all these
rows seems super random. Numbers with lots

00:26:20.150 --> 00:26:28.700
of factors have lots of divisors, primes only
have two divisors, and you might think that

00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:34.230
you’d need perfect knowledge of the distribution
of primes to get anything useful out of this.

00:26:34.230 --> 00:26:40.100
But if instead you organize these into columns,
the puzzle starts to fit together.

00:26:40.100 --> 00:26:46.450
How many numbers between 1 and R2 have 1 as
a divisors; well, all of them, so our sum

00:26:46.450 --> 00:26:53.740
should include R2*chi(1)
How many have 2 as a divisors. Well, about

00:26:53.740 --> 00:26:59.260
half of them, so that accounts for (R2/2)*chi(2)
in our total tally up.

00:26:59.260 --> 00:27:15.799
About a third of the rows have a chi(3), so
that’s (R2/3)*chi(3).

00:27:15.799 --> 00:27:22.070
Keep going like this, and the total count
of lattice points looks like R2*chi(1) +(R2/2)*chi(2)

00:27:22.070 --> 00:27:27.390
+ (R2/3) * chi(3), and so on. Factoring out
that R2, and bringing back the 4 that needs

00:27:27.390 --> 00:27:33.090
to be multiplied in, this means the total
number of lattice points in this big circle

00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:42.900
is approximately 4R2(..this sum..).
Since chi is 0 on all even numbers, and oscillates

00:27:42.900 --> 00:27:53.100
between 1 and -1 for odd numbers, that sum
looks like 1 - ⅓ + ⅕ - 1/7, and so on.

00:27:53.100 --> 00:27:59.480
This is what we wanted! An alternative expression
for the number of lattice points in a big

00:27:59.480 --> 00:28:07.190
circle, which we know to be around pi*R2
The bigger R is, the more accurate both these

00:28:07.190 --> 00:28:14.160
estimates are, so the error between these
sides can be arbitrarily small.

00:28:14.160 --> 00:28:21.640
Dividing by R2, this gives us an infinite
sum which should converge to pi.

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:30.190
And the reason this sum is so simple, just
oscillating back and forth between odd numbers,

00:28:30.190 --> 00:28:37.530
stems from the regular pattern for how prime
numbers factor inside the Gaussian integers.

00:28:37.530 --> 00:28:42.290
If you’re curious, there are two main branches
of number theory: Algebraic number theory,

00:28:42.290 --> 00:28:47.080
and analytic number theory.
Very loosely speaking, the former deals with

00:28:47.080 --> 00:28:53.250
new number systems, like these Gaussian integers
you and I looked at, while the latter deals

00:28:53.250 --> 00:28:58.220
with things like the Riemann zeta function,
or its cousins called “L” functions which

00:28:58.220 --> 00:29:03.290
involve multiplicative functions like the
central character chi from our story.

00:29:03.290 --> 00:29:08.429
The path we just walked is a little glimpse
of where those two fields intersect. Both

00:29:08.429 --> 00:29:13.080
are pretty heavy-duty fields with lots of
active research and unsolved problems. So

00:29:13.080 --> 00:29:17.190
if this feels like something which will take
time to mentally digest, like there’s more

00:29:17.190 --> 00:29:24.110
patterns to be uncovered and understood, it’s
because it is, and there are.

00:29:24.110 --> 00:29:30.780
So, you are all demonstrably the kind of people
